/E (Chen and Verma investigated the implementation of secure authentication mechanisms in web applications using JSON Web Tokens \(JWT\). Their study focused on how JWT can provide stateless, scalable, and tamper-proof authentication for both single-page and multi-page web applications. The authors highlighted the role of token expiration, signature verification, and refresh tokens in maintaining security while improving user experience. The research demonstrated that JWT-based authentication reduces server-side session management overhead and simplifies API security in distributed architectures. However, they noted potential risks, such as token theft and replay attacks, which require additional safeguards like HTTPS enforcement, secure storage, and token revocation strategies.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 267 0 R
/E (Patel and Singh examined the architecture and implementation of database-driven web applications, emphasizing the interaction between front-end interfaces and back-end databases. Their study focused on how dynamic content generation, CRUD operations, and data persistence can be efficiently managed through relational and NoSQL databases. The authors highlighted the significance of database normalization, query optimization, and secure connection handling to ensure performance, scalability, and data integrity. Their findings indicated that database-driven applications significantly improve user interactivity and administrative efficiency compared to static web pages.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
