594 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 269 0 R
/E (One of the major challenges of the existing event management systems is their heavy reliance on manual processes and disconnected digital tools. Activities such as event registration, ticket allocation, and participant tracking are often handled using spreadsheets, paper forms, or separate online platforms. This lack of integration leads to data inconsistency, duplication of records, and difficulty in maintaining accurate event information. Manual intervention at multiple stages increases the possibility of errors and delays, especially when managing large-scale events with a high number of participants.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 269 0 R
/E (In the existing system, event management is primarily carried out using manual methods or basic digital tools such as spreadsheets, emails, and standalone registration forms. These approaches require significant human involvement for tasks such as event scheduling, participant registration, and ticket distribution. As a result, the process becomes time-consuming and prone to errors such as duplicate entries, incorrect data handling, and loss of important information. Managing large-scale events using such methods often leads to confusion and inefficient coordination among organizers.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
596 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 269 0 R
/E (Additionally, existing systems lack centralized control and real-time data updates. Event details and participant information are often scattered across multiple platforms, making monitoring and management difficult. The absence of proper role separation between administrators and users increases the risk of unauthorized access and data misuse. Due to limited security features and poor scalability, existing systems are unable to efficiently handle increasing user demand and multiple concurrent events.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
